== Jitter RNG <sec:options-jitter-rng>

#option-entry("es_jent", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for Jitter RNG entropy source", (("par", "The ESDM may use the Jitter RNG as entropy source. Enabling this option enables the use of the Jitter RNG."),))

#option-entry("es_jent_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "256", "Jitter RNG entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the Jitter RNG entropy source. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be in the range between 0 and 256."),))

#option-entry("es_jent_osr", "integer, 3 to 256", "3", "Jitter RNG entropy source oversampling rate", (("par", "The option defines the oversampling multiplier used in jitterentropy. This is often adapted to get more conservative entropy estimates. Please note, that every increase reduces the output performance of this source due to increased internal sampling rounds."),))

#option-entry("es_jent_ntg1", "feature", "disabled", "Jitter RNG NTG.1 compliance mode", (("par", "The option enables the NTG.1 mode of the jitter RNG (a BSI AIS 20/31 functionality class). This internally also enables FIPS mode and health tests. Please make sure, that you configured jitterentropy according to entropy estimates on your target platform. Otherwise failing health tests may disable the output of this entropy source."), ("par", "The mode requires jitterentropy 3.7.0 or later with support for secure memory. A jitter RNG operated this way is NTG.1 conformant on its own, so the seeding strategy selected with 'ais2031_ntg1' lets it seed a DRNG without a second entropy source next to it.")))

#option-entry("es_jent_max_mem", "integer, -1 to 20", "-1", "Jitter RNG max memory buffer size", (("par", "The jitter RNG performs random accesses on a memory buffer as part of its noise source. Use this option to adapt the size of the buffer after entropy measurements with different memory sizes. Please consult the source code of your jitterentropy version for a current meaning of the values. At the time of writing this description text e.g. 16 would allocate 32 MB."),))

#option-entry("es_jent_hash_loop_count", "integer, -1 to 7", "-1", "Jitter RNG hash loop count", (("par", "The jitter RNG performs SHA3-256 iterations as part of its noise source. Use this option to adapt the number of iterations. Please consult the source code of your jitterentropy version for a current meaning of the values. At the time of writing this description text e.g. 3 would iterate 8 times."),))

#option-entry("es_jent_all_caches", "feature", "disabled", "Jitter RNG cache logic use all caches", (("par", "If no max memory size is given, the jitter RNG estimates an adequate size of the memory buffer depending on the cache size of the system. Typically only the size of the L1 is used for this. With this option, the sum all caches up to L3 is taken into account."),))

#option-entry("es_jent_instances", "integer, 0 to 64", "0", "Jitter RNG collectors working side by side", (("par", "Each collector serves DRNG seedings behind a lock of its own. Collecting dominates a seeding, so a build reseeding often (sp80090c, ais2031_drg4) scales with the CPUs for the collectors, at a collector's memory each. The default for esdm_config_es_jent_instances_set() and esdm-server --es_jent_instances; 0 picks 4 for a build reseeding often and 1 otherwise."),))

== CPU Entropy Source <sec:options-cpu-entropy-source>

#option-entry("es_cpu", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for CPU-based entropy source", (("par", "Current CPUs commonly contain entropy sources which can be used to seed the ESDM. For example, the Intel RDSEED instruction, or the POWER DARN instruction will be sourced to seed the ESDM if this option is enabled."), ("par", "Note, if this option is enabled and the underlying CPU does not offer such entropy source, the ESDM will automatically detect this and ignore the hardware.")))

#option-entry("es_cpu_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "8", "CPU-based entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the CPU entropy source. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be in the range between 0 and 256."), ("par", "When configuring this value to 0, the CPU entropy source will provide 256 bits of data without being credited to contain entropy.")))

#option-entry("es_cpu_multiplier", "integer, 1 to …", "1", "CPU-based entropy source data multiplier", (("par", "Some entropy sources specify that its entropy source requires an oversampling. This multiplier is set to a default not performing an oversampling. However, this value is adjusted by specific CPU entropy source glue code."),))

== Kernel RNG Entropy Source <sec:options-kernel-rng-entropy-source>

#option-entry("es_kernel", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for kernel entropy source (/dev/random)", ())

#option-entry("es_kernel_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "128", "Kernel entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the kernel RNG entropy source. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be in the range between 0 and 256."), ("par", "When configuring this value to 0, the kernel RNG entropy source will provide 256 bits of data without being credited to contain entropy."), ("par", "Note: This value is set to 0 automatically when booting the kernel in FIPS mode (with fips=1 kernel command line option). This is due to the fact that random.c is not SP800-90B compliant.")))

== Interrupt Entropy Source <sec:options-interrupt-entropy-source>

#option-entry("es_irq", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for interrupt-based entropy source", (("par", "The ESDM models an entropy source based on the timing of the occurrence of interrupts. Enable this option to enable this IRQ entropy source."), ("par", "The IRQ entropy source is triggered every time an interrupt arrives and thus causes the interrupt handler to execute slightly longer. Disabling the IRQ entropy source implies that the performance penalty on the interrupt handler added by the ESDM is eliminated. Yet, this entropy source is considered to be an internal entropy source of the ESDM. Thus, only disable it if you ensured that other entropy sources are available that supply the ESDM with entropy."), ("par", "Side note: when enabling this entropy source, one of the main entropy sources of the kernel RNG is diverted into ESDM. This means that this entropy source is not available to the kernel RNG any more. To offset the issue, the ESDM reseeds the kernel RNG once in a while.")))

#option-entry("es_irq_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "256", "Interrupt-based entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "ES configuration done in the kernel module - see kernel module code."), ("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the interrupt entropy source. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be between 0 and 256 bits. Note, this entropy rate is applied on the entropy value reported by the interrupt-ES kernel module."), ("par", "Note, this value caps the interrupt ES kernel module value!"), ("par", "Note 2: The events for the interrupt-based and scheduler-based ES both have some dependencies. Thus it is not permissible to award both entropy sources an entropy rate greater than zero.")))

== Scheduler Entropy Source <sec:options-scheduler-entropy-source>

#option-entry("es_sched", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for scheduler-based entropy source", (("par", "The ESDM models an entropy source based on the timing of task wakeups. Enable this option to enable this scheduler entropy source."), ("par", "The scheduler entropy source is triggered every time a task is woken up and thus causes the scheduler to execute slightly longer. Disabling the scheduler entropy source implies that the performance penalty on the scheduler added by the ESDM is eliminated. Yet, this entropy source is considered to be an internal entropy source of the ESDM. Thus, only disable it if you ensured that other entropy sources are available that supply the ESDM with entropy."), ("par", "If you disable the scheduler entropy source, you MUST ensure one or more entropy sources collectively have the capability to deliver sufficient entropy with one invocation at a rate compliant to the security strength of the DRNG (usually 256 bits of entropy). In addition, if those entropy sources do not deliver sufficient entropy during first request, the reseed must be triggered from user space or kernel space when sufficient entropy is considered to be present.")))

#option-entry("es_sched_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "0", "Scheduler-based entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "ES configuration done in the kernel module - see kernel module code."), ("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the scheduler entropy source. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be between 0 and 256 bits. Note, this entropy rate is applied on the entropy value reported by the scheduler-ES kernel module."), ("par", "Note, this value caps the scheduler ES kernel module value!"), ("par", "Note 2: The events for the interrupt-based and scheduler-based ES both have some dependencies. Thus it is not permissible to award both entropy sources an entropy rate greater than zero.")))

== eBPF Entropy Sources <sec:options-ebpf-entropy-sources>

#option-entry("es_sched_ebpf", "feature", "disabled", "Enable support for eBPF-based scheduler entropy source", (("par", "The ESDM models an entropy source based on the timing of the occurrence of scheduler-triggered context switches. In contrast to the es_sched entropy source which requires a patched kernel, this entropy source obtains the context switch timing by attaching an eBPF program to the stable sched_switch tracepoint of a stock Linux kernel (BTF/CO-RE required, i.e. the kernel must be built with CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF)."), ("par", "The event timestamps are health-tested (SP800-90B RCT and APT) inside the eBPF program, which collects the time between successive events of a CPU in per-CPU buffers - the spacing of the events is where the entropy is, and it is the signal the SP800-90B assessment measures. A buffer is handed to a BPF ring buffer once it is full, or after an idle deadline when its CPU falls quiet before that. The ESDM server reads them from there when it wants entropy, conditions them in an XDRBG-256 pool and stops reading once that pool is full. Events collected while nobody reads are discarded."), ("par", "This entropy source is mutually exclusive with the es_sched and es_irq entropy sources with respect to entropy crediting: at most one of the scheduler/interrupt-based entropy sources may have an entropy rate greater than zero."), ("par", "The BPF programs are loaded during the privileged initialization phase of the ESDM server (root or CAP_BPF + CAP_PERF_MON required).")))

#option-entry("es_sched_ebpf_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "0", "eBPF scheduler-based entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the eBPF scheduler entropy source. Every context switch event contributes its 64 bit time delta, so 256 bits of data are four events. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be between 0 and 256 bits."), ("par", "The credited entropy is additionally capped by the oversampling rate (es_sched_ebpf_osr), which states the assessed min-entropy of one event."), ("par", "The default of 0 means the entropy source is not credited with any entropy until the entropy rate of the deployment environment has been measured with the es_ebpf_testing support and a suitable rate has been determined."), ("par", "Note: The events for the interrupt-based and scheduler-based entropy sources have dependencies. Thus it is not permissible to award more than one of the es_irq, es_sched, es_irq_ebpf and es_sched_ebpf entropy sources an entropy rate greater than zero.")))

#option-entry("es_sched_ebpf_osr", "integer, 1 to 256", "1", "eBPF scheduler-based entropy source oversampling rate", (("par", "The option defines the oversampling rate the SP800-90B health tests of the eBPF scheduler entropy source are parameterized with: the tests assume a min-entropy of H = 1 / OSR bits per event, i.e. OSR events are required per bit of entropy. It determines the RCT and APT cutoffs applied inside the eBPF program."), ("par", "Set it from the min-entropy measured with the es_ebpf_testing support: OSR = ceil(1 / H), rounded up so that the cutoffs are never less strict than the measurement warrants. The value must be consistent with es_sched_ebpf_entropy_rate: as 256 bits of data are four events, a rate of 4 / OSR bits per 256 data bits is the most the assessment supports. The accounting caps anything above that and the server logs a warning."), ("par", "The default of 1 is the strictest setting (one bit of entropy per event) and matches the shipped entropy rate of 0, which credits no entropy at all.")))

#option-entry("es_irq_ebpf", "feature", "disabled", "Enable support for eBPF-based interrupt entropy source", (("par", "The ESDM models an entropy source based on the timing of the occurrence of interrupts. In contrast to the es_irq entropy source which requires a patched kernel, this entropy source obtains the interrupt timing by attaching an eBPF program to the stable irq_handler_entry tracepoint of a stock Linux kernel (BTF/CO-RE required, i.e. the kernel must be built with CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF)."), ("par", "Note: unlike the es_irq entropy source, this entropy source does not divert the interrupt timings from the kernel RNG - it only observes them. Both the kernel RNG and this entropy source therefore process the same events. For that reason the entropy rate of the kernel RNG entropy source is capped when this entropy source is credited with entropy, and the default entropy rate is 0 (the data is mixed in as uncredited bonus data)."), ("par", "This entropy source is mutually exclusive with the es_sched and es_irq entropy sources with respect to entropy crediting: at most one of the scheduler/interrupt-based entropy sources may have an entropy rate greater than zero.")))

#option-entry("es_irq_ebpf_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "0", "eBPF interrupt-based entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the eBPF interrupt entropy source. Every interrupt event contributes its 64 bit time delta, so 256 bits of data are four events. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be between 0 and 256 bits."), ("par", "The credited entropy is additionally capped by the oversampling rate (es_irq_ebpf_osr), which states the assessed min-entropy of one event."), ("par", "The default of 0 means the data of this entropy source is mixed in as uncredited bonus data. This is the recommended setting because the kernel RNG processes the same interrupt events."), ("par", "Note: The events for the interrupt-based and scheduler-based entropy sources have dependencies. Thus it is not permissible to award more than one of the es_irq, es_sched, es_irq_ebpf and es_sched_ebpf entropy sources an entropy rate greater than zero.")))

#option-entry("es_irq_ebpf_osr", "integer, 1 to 256", "1", "eBPF interrupt-based entropy source oversampling rate", (("par", "The option defines the oversampling rate the SP800-90B health tests of the eBPF interrupt entropy source are parameterized with: the tests assume a min-entropy of H = 1 / OSR bits per event, i.e. OSR events are required per bit of entropy. It determines the RCT and APT cutoffs applied inside the eBPF program."), ("par", "Set it from the min-entropy measured with the es_ebpf_testing support: OSR = ceil(1 / H), rounded up so that the cutoffs are never less strict than the measurement warrants. The value must be consistent with es_irq_ebpf_entropy_rate: as 256 bits of data are four events, a rate of 4 / OSR bits per 256 data bits is the most the assessment supports. The accounting caps anything above that and the server logs a warning."), ("par", "The default of 1 is the strictest setting (one bit of entropy per event) and matches the shipped entropy rate of 0, which credits no entropy at all.")))

#option-entry("es_ebpf_testing", "feature", "disabled", "Enable raw measurement support of the eBPF entropy sources", (("par", "This option enables the raw event timestamp recording support in the eBPF programs and builds the esdm-ebpf-collect tool, which gathers the raw, unconditioned time between events as decimal ASCII for SP800-90B entropy assessment. Enable validation-helpers alongside it for esdm-extractlsb, which turns that into the byte stream the NIST tools read."), ("par", "THIS OPTION IS FOR MEASUREMENT AND VALIDATION ONLY - DO NOT ENABLE IN PRODUCTION BUILDS. The collection tool refuses to operate when the kernel FIPS mode is enabled. The ESDM server itself never records raw data.")))

== Hardware RNG Entropy Source <sec:options-hardware-rng-entropy-source>

#option-entry("es_hwrand", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for /dev/hwrng-based entropy source", (("par", "The Linux kernel offers the hardware random number generator framework which is accessible via /dev/hwrng. This interface covers also TPM2.0 devices."), ("par", "Note, if this option is enabled and the underlying Linux system does not offer such entropy source, the ESDM will automatically detect this and ignore the hardware.")))

#option-entry("es_hwrand_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "0", "/dev/hwrng entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the /dev/hwrng entropy source. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be in the range between 0 and 256."), ("par", "When configuring this value to 0, the default, the /dev/hwrng entropy source provides 256 bits of data without being credited to contain entropy. A seeding then skips the device while another uses it: waiting would serialize every seeding.")))

== TPM 2.0 Entropy Source <sec:options-tpm-2-0-entropy-source>

#option-entry("es_tpm2", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for TPM 2.0 entropy source", (("par", "The ESDM may use a TPM 2.0 chip as entropy source. Enabling this option enables the use of the TPM 2.0 random number generator to seed the ESDM."), ("par", "Note, if this option is enabled and the underlying system does not offer a TPM 2.0 chip, the ESDM will automatically detect this and ignore the hardware.")))

#option-entry("es_tpm2_stir", "feature", "disabled", "Enable TPM 2.0 stir support", (("par", "When enabled, the ESDM forces the internal TPM 2.0 DRBG to reseed from its hardware entropy source before obtaining random data. This ensures that fresh entropy is used but is typically very slow. Only enable this if you require the additional assurance of a freshly reseeded TPM 2.0 DRBG."),))

#option-entry("es_tpm2_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "0", "TPM 2.0 entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the TPM 2.0 entropy source. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be in the range between 0 and 256."), ("par", "When configuring this value to 0, the default, the TPM 2.0 entropy source provides 256 bits of data without being credited to contain entropy. A seeding then skips the TPM while another uses it: it serves one command at a time, and waiting would serialize every seeding.")))

#option-entry("es_tpm2_rm_path", "string", "/dev/tpmrm0", "TPM 2.0 resource manager device path", (("par", "The option defines the path to the TPM 2.0 resource manager device used by the ESDM to access the TPM 2.0 chip. The default path is /dev/tpmrm0 which is the standard Linux TPM resource manager device."), ("par", "Adjust this path if your system provides the TPM 2.0 resource manager at a different location.")))

== PKCS#11 and PC/SC Entropy Sources <sec:options-pkcs-11-and-pc-sc-entropy-sources>

#option-entry("es_pkcs11", "feature", "disabled", "Build esdm-es-pkcs11, the PKCS#11 entropy source", (("par", "esdm-es-pkcs11 serves the random number generator of a PKCS#11 token (e.g. a smart card or HSM, C_GenerateRandom through libp11) to the ESDM as an external entropy source (es_ext) - see esdm-es-pkcs11(8). Neither libesdm nor esdm-server links libp11. Needs esdm-es-ext. The options below are defaults of the daemon; its PIN is read from a file or a systemd credential."),))

#option-entry("es_pkcs11_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "128", "Entropy rate esdm-es-pkcs11 declares, per 256 bits of data", (("par", "The ESDM credits it unless es_ext_entropy_rate configures another one."),))

#option-entry("es_pkcs11_module_path", "string", "(empty)", "PKCS#11 module esdm-es-pkcs11 loads by default", (("par", "Path of the PKCS#11 module shared library to load (e.g. /usr/lib/softhsm/libsofthsm2.so or /usr/lib/pkcs11/opensc-pkcs11.so). Empty makes its --module option a requirement."),))

#option-entry("es_pkcs11_token_label", "string", "(empty)", "PKCS#11 token label esdm-es-pkcs11 looks for by default", (("par", "If set, only a token whose label matches this value will be used. Otherwise the first slot with a present token that exposes an RNG is selected."),))

#option-entry("es_pcsc", "feature", "disabled", "Build esdm-es-pcsc, the PC/SC smart card entropy source", (("par", "esdm-es-pcsc serves the GET CHALLENGE of a smart card (ISO/IEC 7816-4, over pcsc-lite) to the ESDM as an external entropy source (es_ext). It declares no entropy rate unless started with --entropy_rate. Needs esdm-es-ext."),))

