#option-entry("es_jent_kernel", "feature", "disabled", "Enable support for the in-kernel jitterentropy-based entropy source", ())

#option-entry("es_jent_kernel_name", "string", "jitterentropy_rng", "Kernel crypto API RNG of the kernel jitterentropy source", (("par", "The name, or the driver name, of the RNG of /proc/crypto the source reads through AF_ALG: jitterentropy_rng is the one of the kernel. A jitterentropy kernel module registering its RNG under another name - such as the module of jitterentropy 3.8.0 - is selected by its name here, or with esdm-server --es_jent_kernel_name NAME."),))

#option-entry("es_jent_kernel_entropy_rate", "integer, 0 to 256", "256", "kernel-based jitter entropy source entropy rate", (("par", "The option defines the amount of entropy the ESDM applies to 256 bits of data obtained from the kernel jitter entropy source. The ESDM enforces the limit that this value must be in the range between 0 and 256."), ("par", "When configuring this value to 0, the kernel jitter entropy source will provide 256 bits of data without being credited to contain entropy.")))

== External Entropy Sources <sec:options-external-entropy-sources>

#option-entry("es_ext", "feature", "enabled", "Enable the external entropy source", (("par", "An external entropy source is served by a provider: a process of its own the ESDM reads over a Unix domain socket - built with libesdm_es_ext, or speaking the protocol of esdm_es_ext.h itself. It is called ExternalSocket in the ESDM status, and does nothing until a socket is configured (es_ext_path, esdm-server --es_ext)."),))

#option-entry("es_ext_path", "string", "(empty)", "Socket of the external entropy source provider", (("par", "esdm-server --es_ext overrides it. The ESDM connects on demand, and again whenever the connection is lost."),))

#option-entry("es_ext_entropy_rate", "integer, -1 to 256", "-1", "Entropy rate cap of the external entropy source", (("par", "A block is credited with the entropy the provider claims, at most at this rate per 256 bits of data. -1 configures none: the rate the provider declares, else 0. Accepting the user a provider runs as (es_ext_peer_uid) means trusting the rate it declares - configure one here otherwise. esdm-server --es_ext_entropy_rate overrides it."),))

#option-entry("es_ext_peer_uid", "integer, -1 to 4294967294", "-1", "User the external entropy source provider may run as", (("par", "Whoever can create the socket file could otherwise feed the ESDM data credited as entropy: a provider running as root is accepted, and one running as this uid; -1 accepts root only. esdm-server --es_ext_uid overrides it."),))

== Seeding, Compliance and Threads <sec:options-seeding-compliance-and-threads>

#option-entry("node", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for multiple DRNG nodes", ())

#option-entry("fips140", "boolean", "false", "Enable FIPS 140 support", (("par", "This option implicitly enables the SP800-90C compliance mode, and with it the build adjustments of the sp80090c option: the reseeding limits apply."),))

#option-entry("sp80090c", "boolean", "false", "SP800-90C compliance", (("par", "If enabled, the ESDM oversamples the entropy sources compliant to SP800-90C."), ("par", "The following oversampling is applied:"), ("item", "Seed the DRNG with 128 more bits of entropy from the conditioning component."), ("item", "Inject 64 more bits of entropy from the noise source into the conditioning component.")))

#option-entry("ais2031_ntg1", "boolean", "false", "German AIS 20/31 3.0 (2024) NTG.1 seeding strategy", (("par", "When enabling this option, two entropy sources must deliver 240 bits of entropy each to consider a DRNG as fully seeded. Any two entropy sources can be used to fulfill this requirement. If specific entropy sources shall not be capable of contributing to this seeding strategy, the respective entropy source must be configured to provide less than 240 bits of entropy."), ("par", "The strategy is consistent with the requirements for NTG.1 compliance in German AIS 20/31 3.0 from 2024."), ("par", "The requirement of two entropy sources does not apply when the jitter RNG is operated in its own NTG.1 mode (option 'es_jent_ntg1' together with jitterentropy 3.7.0 or later). Such a jitter RNG is NTG.1 conformant on its own and therefore seeds a DRNG without a second entropy source; 240 bits of entropy from it are then sufficient."), ("par", "Compliance with German AIS 20/31 from 2011 is always present when using /dev/random with the flag O_SYNC or getrandom(2) with GRND_RANDOM."), ("par", "The DRG.4 reseeding limits of the same standard are configured independently with the option 'ais2031_drg4'.")))

#option-entry("ais2031_drg3", "boolean", "false", "German AIS 20/31 3.0 (2024) DRG.3 class", (("par", "This option applies the entropy source oversampling of the 'sp80090c' option without the request size based reseeding limits that come with it. The reseeding thresholds are left at whatever 'drng_reseed_threshold_bits' and 'drng_max_reseed_bits' configure, and are not capped - those limits belong to DRG.4."), ("par", "Setting 'sp80090c' alongside this option does not bring the limits back."), ("par", "Mutually exclusive with 'ais2031_drg4', which adds the reseeding limits this option leaves out.")))

#option-entry("ais2031_drg4", "boolean", "false", "German AIS 20/31 3.0 (2024) DRG.4 class", (("par", "DRG.4 satisfies DRG.3: this option applies the entropy source oversampling of 'ais2031_drg3' and in addition the request size based reseeding limits of DRG.4."), ("par", "When enabling this option, the request size based reseeding limits are capped to the values required by DRG.4.10 of German AIS 20/31 3.0 from 2024: a DRNG instance starts no request after 2**17 output bits without a successful reseed (one started before is completed, in generate operations of 2**19 bits at most) and tries to reseed after three quarters of that at the latest."), ("par", "Smaller values configured through 'drng_reseed_threshold_bits' and 'drng_max_reseed_bits' are kept as they are."), ("par", "The NTG.1 seeding strategy of the same standard is configured independently with the option 'ais2031_ntg1'."), ("par", "Mutually exclusive with 'ais2031_drg3'.")))

#option-entry("threading_max_worker_threads", "integer, 1 to …", "64", "Maximum number of concurrent worker threads supported", (("par", "This value can be set to any arbitrary number. Depending on the number of threads, the required numbers of thread contexts are statically allocated."), ("par", "The number of threads define:"), ("item", "the number of concurrent DRNG instances that are maintained independent of each other - this value is limited by the number of found CPUs as it makes no sense to have more DRNG instances than CPUs that can execute them."), ("par", "There is no other value that needs changing if the number of threads shall be adjusted."), ("par", "The value must not be lower than 1.")))

#option-entry("es_parallel_fetch", "feature", "enabled", "Fetch seed material from entropy sources in parallel", (("par", "When enabled, the entropy source manager spawns one thread per entropy source to gather seed material concurrently during a reseed, joining them afterwards. This reduces reseed latency when slow entropy sources (e.g. TPM 2.0 or PKCS#11 tokens) are configured."), ("par", "Parallel fetching is only used at runtime if more than one processor core is available; otherwise the sources are queried sequentially. If thread creation fails for an individual source, that source is queried inline as a fallback."), ("par", "When disabled, the entropy sources are always queried sequentially.")))

#option-entry("drng_reseed_threshold_bits", "integer", "0xffffffff", "Output bits after which a DRNG instance tries to reseed", (("par", "Besides request count based reseeding, this option can be used to enable request size (Bit) based reseeding. Set this to UINT32_MAX (0xffffffff) to disable it (default)."), ("par", "Request size based reseeding works in two stages. After 'drng_reseed_threshold_bits' (this option) the DRNG code tries to reseed the DRNG instance."), ("par", "If this was unsuccessful multiple times, the DRNG changes into the unseeded state after 'drng_max_reseed_bits' bits. Therefore 'drng_max_reseed_bits' always has to be larger than 'drng_reseed_threshold_bits'."), ("par", "Set this setting to anything lower than 131072 (2**17) to enforce DRG.4.10 of AIS 20/31 Version 3.0. E.g. 65536 (2**16) and 'drng_max_reseed_bits' to 131072 (2**17)."), ("par", "When the options sp80090c, fips140 and/or ais2031_drg4 are defined, 'drng_max_reseed_bits' is capped at 131072 (2**17) and this option at three quarters of it, 98304 bits, unless smaller values are configured. ais2031_drg3 leaves both uncapped.")))

#option-entry("drng_max_reseed_bits", "integer", "0xffffffff", "Output bits after which a DRNG instance is no longer fully seeded", (("par", "This sets the maximum number of output/request bits before the DRNG instances lose their fully seeded state without successful reseeding."), ("par", "UINT32_MAX (0xffffffff) disables this setting. For more details on this setting see the description of 'drng_reseed_threshold_bits'.")))

#option-entry("num-aux-pools", "integer, 1 to 1024", "1", "Number of auxiliary pools", (("par", "More pools make the ESDM more robust on seed bursts."),))

#option-entry("aux-has-full-entropy", "feature", "disabled", "Treat the data inserted into the auxiliary pool as having full entropy", (("par", "The auxiliary entropy source applies oversampling, if SP800-90C mode is enabled, because it has to assume by default that its inputs did not have full entropy (small error term allowed)."), ("par", "Enable this option to turn the oversampling off, if e.g. only output from an approved smartcard is inserted into the pool and you know it has full entropy.")))

#option-entry("systemd", "feature", "enabled", "Enable support for systemd notify and systemd socket activation", (("par", "ESDM services will notify systemd about being ready and use provided FDs for their sockets (socket activation)."),))

== Cryptographic Backends <sec:options-cryptographic-backends>

#option-entry("drng_hash_drbg", "feature", "enabled", "Builtin: SP800-90A Hash Deterministic Random Number Generator", (("par", "This configuration enables an SP800-90A Hash DRBG with SHA-512 core without prediction resistance when the builtin crypto primitives are selected."),))

#option-entry("drng_chacha20", "feature", "disabled", "Builtin: ChaCha20-based Deterministic Random Number Generator", ())

#option-entry("hash_sha512", "feature", "enabled", "Builtin: Enable SHA2-512 conditioning hash", ())

#option-entry("hash_sha3_512", "feature", "disabled", "Builtin: Enable SHA3-512 conditioning hash", ())

#option-entry("crypto_backend", "one of builtin, leancrypto, openssl, botan, gnutls", "builtin", "Select the cryptographic backend", (("par", "The cryptographic backend provides the cryptographic primitives used by the ESDM for the conditioning operation as well as random number generation. Only one backend can be enabled."), ("par", "Note on 'gnutls': its SP800-90A CTR DRBG is operated without a derivation function. Seed material must therefore be provided as full-entropy input of exactly the DRBG seed length - which is what ESDM feeds it - but it does mean this backend has a narrower input contract than the others.")))

#option-entry("openssl_drng_type", "one of ctr, hash, hmac", "hash", "Selects the OpenSSL DRNG implementation", (("par", "In addition to the Hash DRBG, OpenSSL supports a faster CTR DRBG and a more conservative HMAC-DRBG implementation, which conforms to SP800-90A. Default to the middle ground option 'hash'."),))

#option-entry("botan_drng_type", "one of chacha20, hmac", "hmac", "Selects the botan DRNG implementation", (("par", "Botan supports a faster ChaCha20 DRNG and a more conservative HMAC-DRBG implementation, which conforms to SP800-90A. Default to the conservative option 'hmac'."), ("par", "With Botan 3.14.0 or later, the ChaCha20 DRNG runs with fast key erasure: its key is overwritten after each request, which gives its state backtracking resistance (AIS 20/31 DRG.3).")))

== Linux Interfaces and the Server <sec:options-linux-interfaces-and-the-server>

#option-entry("selinux", "feature", "enabled", "Enable SELinux labeling support", (("par", "On some Linux environments, SELinux is used to label the /dev/random and /dev/urandom device files. This allows specially restricted applications, such as the OpenSSH daemon to access those device files."), ("par", "When creating the /dev/random and /dev/urandom devices, the SELinux support of this option is used to ensure both are labeled identically to the original device files.")))

#option-entry("linux-devfiles", "feature", "enabled", "Enable the Linux /dev/random and /dev/urandom support", (("par", "Using CUSE, the device files are provided."),))

#option-entry("linux-getrandom", "feature", "enabled", "Enable the Linux getrandom system call support", ())

#option-entry("linux-getrandom-num-nodes", "integer, 0 to 64", "1", "Number of DRNG nodes to allocate for getrandom. 0 means no limit", ())

#option-entry("openssl-rand-provider", "feature", "disabled", "Enable the OpenSSL >= 3 RAND/SEED-SRC provider support", (("par", "Please note, that this is a ESDM frontend, which can provide random numbers originated in ESDM to applications based on OpenSSL. Use -Dcrypto_backend=openssl if you want to use the OpenSSL as a provider for ESDM-internal crypto algorithms, like hash algorithms."),))

#option-entry("linux-reseed-interval", "integer", "120", "interval between forced Linux kernel RNG reseeds (in seconds)", ())

#option-entry("linux-reseed-entropy-count", "integer, 0 to 512", "0", "amount of entropy to account on kernel reseed (without es_irq)", ())

#option-entry("esdm-server", "feature", "enabled", "Enable the ESDM server", ())

#option-entry("esdm-server-rpc", "feature", "enabled", "Build the RPC interface of the ESDM server and its clients", (("par", "The esdm-server serves the RPC interface on its two sockets, the unprivileged and the privileged one. Disabled, the interface is not part of the build: the server contains no RPC code and opens neither socket, and libesdm_rpc_client and what needs it are not built - the kernel seeder, the RPC based OpenSSL provider modules, the RPC socket units and the tests of the interface. linux-devfiles and linux-getrandom need the interface and have to be disabled with it."), ("par", "The server then serves the EGD interface only: on the sockets it is given, or on the socket of esdm-server-egd-socket-path where it is not given the regular socket (a build with testmode uses /tmp/esdm-egd-testmode.socket there). esdm-tool and the resume notification of esdm-server-signal-helper work over that interface. With esdm-server enabled, at least one of esdm-server-rpc and esdm-server-egd has to be enabled.")))

#option-entry("esdm-server-egd", "feature", "enabled", "Build the EGD interface of the ESDM server and its clients", (("par", "The esdm-server serves the EGD protocol with the extensions of the ESDM on the sockets given with --egd_socket and --egd_socket_pr or handed over by the EGD socket units. Disabled, the interface is not part of the build: the server contains no EGD code and rejects the two options, and libesdm_egd_client, the EGD based OpenSSL provider modules, the EGD socket units and the tests of the interface are not built. The kernel module esdm_cdev is built on its own and reads the ESDM through this interface: it needs a server built with it."),))

#option-entry("esdm-server-rpc-path-unprivileged", "string", "/run", "Sets the base path of ESDM's RPC sockets (default: /run)", (("par", "If this value is changed from the default, custom systemd units need to be adapted accordingly by the integrator of ESDM (if systemd is used at all)."), ("par", "Use this e.g. combination with chroot to create a directory with only the unprivileged endpoint.")))

#option-entry("esdm-server-egd-socket-path", "string", "/run/esdm-egd.socket", "Path of the optional EGD compatibility socket (default: /run/esdm-egd.socket)", (("par", "The path is used by the esdm-server-egd.socket systemd unit. The EGD interface is opt-in: it is only served when that socket unit is started or when the esdm-server is invoked with --egd_socket <path>, so this option merely defines where the socket unit places it."), ("par", "A server built without the RPC interface (esdm-server-rpc disabled) serves the EGD interface in any case: on this path when it is given no socket, and esdm-tool of such a build talks to this path.")))

#option-entry("esdm-server-egd-pr-socket-path", "string", "/run/esdm-egd-pr.socket", "Path of the optional prediction resistance EGD socket", (("par", "Counterpart of esdm-server-egd-socket-path serving the prediction resistance generator. The EGD protocol cannot ask for prediction resistance per request, so it is a property of the socket: everything arriving here is answered from esdm_get_random_bytes_pr()."),))

#option-entry("esdm-server-rpc-path-privileged", "string", "/run", "Sets the base path of ESDM's RPC sockets (default: /run)", (("par", "If this value is changed from the default, custom systemd units need to be adapted accordingly by the integrator of ESDM (if systemd is used at all)."), ("par", "You may use this option to keep the privileged socket in a different path than the unprivileged socket.")))

== Clients <sec:options-clients>

#option-entry("esdm-aux-client", "feature", "enabled", "Enable the ESDM auxiliary client library", (("par", "With this option, the ESDM auxiliary client library is enabled allowing access to auxiliary functions offered by the ESDM. This library is a convenience wrapper around interfaces offered by ESDM that can be directly used."),))

#option-entry("esdm-es-ext", "feature", "enabled", "Enable the external entropy source library", (("par", "libesdm_es_ext and esdm_es_ext.h are what an external entropy source (es_ext) is developed against - see libesdm_es_ext(3)."),))

#option-entry("client-connect-timeout-exponent", "integer, 20 to 29", "28", "Timeout for ESDM client/server connection", (("par", "Set the rpc client connect timeout to 1 << VAL nanoseconds. Change the default with care/know the consequences! (perform tests under load, ...)"),))

#option-entry("client-rx-tx-timeout-exponent", "integer, 20 to 29", "28", "Timeout for ESDM client/server rx/tx operations", (("par", "Set the rpc client send/receive timeout to 1 << VAL nanoseconds. Change the default with care/know the consequences! (perform tests under load, ...)"),))

#option-entry("client-reconnect-attempts", "integer, 5 to 30", "10", "Reconnect tries for ESDM client/server connection", (("par", "With this option, you have the possibility to increase the reconnect attempts when decreasing the client connection timeout. Change the default with care/know the consequences! (perform tests under load, ...)"),))

#option-entry("esdm-tool", "feature", "enabled", "Enable the ESDM client CLI tool", (("par", "esdm-tool works over the RPC interface of the server. In a build without it (esdm-server-rpc disabled) it works over the EGD interface and refuses the commands the EGD protocol cannot express - see esdm-tool(1)."),))

== Server <sec:options-server>

#option-entry("esdm-server-rpc-idle-timeout-usec", "integer", "2000000", "ESDM-Server: idle connection timeout", (("par", "Defines the period of time, in which idle/stale RPC sockets are kept in a server thread. The client code also uses this value to close idle connections after half of this time in order to reduce timeouts."), ("par", "Lower this value further, when the amount of worker threads shall be not increased further and a high rate of short connections may saturate them.")))

== Auxiliary Programs <sec:options-auxiliary-programs>

#option-entry("validation-helpers", "feature", "disabled", "Enabled build of entropy validation helpers for esdm_es", ())

#option-entry("esdm-cdev-check", "feature", "disabled", "Install esdm-cdev-check, the checks of the esdm_cdev kernel module", (("par", "The checks of kernel/esdm_cdev are built with the test suite either way, and meson test runs them where the module is loaded. This installs them as well, for a machine without a build tree that loads the module - the NixOS VM test of the flake runs them all from the installed esdm-cdev-check."),))

== Testing <sec:options-testing>

#option-entry("testmode", "feature", "disabled", "Enable test configuration", (("par", "This option enables various test configurations for functional verification testing."), ("par", "WARNING: DO NOT ENABLE FOR PRODUCTION MODE!")))

#option-entry("memory-debug", "feature", "disabled", "Check and annotate memory allocations for debugging purposes", ())

== Fuzzing <sec:options-fuzzing>

#option-entry("fuzzing", "feature", "disabled", "Build the fuzz harnesses in tests/fuzz", (("par", "The harnesses drive the RPC interfaces with attacker-controlled bytes: the requests the server takes off its two sockets, the responses a client parses, and the wire codec underneath both. Building them adds entry points that hand a buffer to the request and response handling without a connection behind it, which is why this is off unless it is asked for."), ("par", "With openssl-rand-provider enabled, each shipped provider module gets a harness as well - there the bytes come from the other direction too, as the caller of a provider is libcrypto and everything it hands over is somebody else's."), ("par", "With a compiler that offers libFuzzer (clang) each harness is built as a fuzzer binary; with any compiler they are also built with a driver that replays their seeds, and that one runs as part of the test suite. Combine with -Db_sanitize=address,undefined - a fuzzer without sanitizers only finds the crashes that are already fatal.")))
